## Core Components

### Tensor

The fundamental data structure. All tensors store data as a flat `[]f32` slice with a
separate `Shape` struct holding the dimension list.

```zig
// Create a 3×4 zero-initialized tensor
var t = try Tensor.zeros(allocator, &[_]usize{ 3, 4 });
defer t.deinit();

// Fill with Xavier/Normal initialization
try t.fillRandomNormal(0.0, 0.02);

// 2D matrix multiply: self = A × B
try result.matmul(&A, &B);

// Spectral norm via power iteration
const sigma = try t.spectralNorm(allocator, 20, 1e-12);

// Binary serialization
try t.save(writer);
var loaded = try Tensor.load(allocator, reader);


Supported operations:

| Method | Description |
|||
| zeros / ones / eye | Standard initializers |
| fillRandomNormal(mean, std) | BoxMuller normal sampling |
| fillRademacher() | ±1 Rademacher random variables |
| matmul(A, B) | Naive O(mnk) matrix multiply |
| outerProduct(other) | Outer product → rank1 matrix |
| normL2() | L2 norm (f64 accumulator) |
| spectralNorm(iters, eps) | Power iteration largest singular value |
| mulScalar(s) / add / sub | Elementwise arithmetic |
| save(writer) / load(reader) | Littleendian binary I/O |
| clone(allocator) | Deep copy |

Precision flags:

zig
pub const Precision = enum { fp4, fp8, fp16, fp32, fp64 };
pub const TensorFlags = struct {
    in_tensor_memory: bool = false,  // Placed in B200 TMEM
    requires_grad:    bool = true,
    is_compressed:    bool = false,   // FP4 compressed
};


Allocator interop:

zig
var t = try Tensor.initWithArena(&arena, &[_]usize{ 512, 512 });
var t = try Tensor.initWithPool(&pool, dims);
var t = try Tensor.initWithBuddy(&buddy, dims);




 SFD Optimizer

Stochastic Fisher Diagonal — an Adam variant augmented with an explicit diagonal Fisher
information matrix estimate.

The update rule per parameter i at step t is:


m_i ← β₁ · m_i + (1 − β₁) · g_i           (momentum)
v_i ← β₂ · v_i + (1 − β₂) · g_i²          (Adam velocity)
F_i ← β₂ · F_i + (1 − β₂) · g_i²          (Fisher diagonal EMA)
F_i ← clamp(F_i, 0, fisher_max)

m̂_i = m_i / (1 − β₁ᵗ)                     (bias correction)
v̂_i = v_i / (1 − β₂ᵗ)

adaptive_lr = lr / (√v̂_i + ε)
update = m̂_i · adaptive_lr / (√F_i + ε)
update = clamp(update, −clip_threshold, +clip_threshold)

θ_i ← θ_i − update


Configuration:

zig
const config = SFDConfig{
    .beta1            = 0.9,
    .beta2            = 0.999,
    .eps              = 1e-8,
    .clip_threshold   = 1.0,
    .fisher_max       = 1e6,
    .warmup_steps     = 10,
    .finite_diff_eps  = 1e-5,
    .second_order_eps = 1e-4,
};

var optimizer = try SFD.initWithConfig(allocator, param_size, config);
defer optimizer.deinit();


Step:

zig
try optimizer.update(&gradients, &params, learning_rate);


Advanced utilities:

zig
// Gradient norm clipping across multiple tensors
const total_norm = optimizer.clipGradNorm(grad_tensors, max_norm);

// Spectral clip a weight matrix (top singular value ≤ max_eig)
try optimizer.spectralClip(&weight_tensor, max_eig);

// Fisher accumulation from a batch of gradient samples
try optimizer.accumulateFisher(grad_batch);
optimizer.resetFisher();

// Warm-start from a previous checkpoint's Fisher diagonal
optimizer.warmStart(&prev_fisher_diag);

// SVRG-style variance reduction
try optimizer.varianceReduction(noisy_grad_batch);

// Cosine annealing LR factor (for AMP-style scheduling)
const factor = optimizer.ampSchedule(step, warmup, total_steps);

// Checkpoint
try optimizer.saveState("checkpoint.bin");
try optimizer.loadState("checkpoint.bin");




 SophiaSOAP Optimizer

The flagship optimizer. Combines five techniques:

| Component | Source Technique | Role |
||||
| SFD | Adam + Fisher | Base adaptive optimizer |
| KFACBlock | Shampoo / SOAP | Kroneckerfactored preconditioner |
| GradientFlowController | SpectralNorm + Gradient Norm Clipping | Stability |
| MARSVarianceReducer | SVRG / MARS | Gradient variance reduction |
| ReversibleOptimizerState | RevNet | Memoryefficient backprop |

Additionally, the Sophia component estimates the Hessian diagonal via Hutchinson's
stochastic estimator using finite differences and uses it to clip the gradient step size.

Initialization:

zig
// layerdims: per-layer [input_dim, output_dim] pairs
const layer_dims = [_]usize{
    [_]usize{ 512, 512 },
    [_]usize{ 512, 256 },
};

const sophia_config = SophiaSOAPConfig{
    .rho                  = 0.04,   // Hessian clipping threshold
    .gamma                = 0.01,   // Minimum Hessian diagonal
    .hessian_update_freq  = 10,     // Steps between Hessian updates
    .use_gauss_newton     = true,   // Gauss-Newton vs. full Hessian
};

var opt = try SophiaSOAPOptimizer.init(
    allocator,
    param_size,
    &layer_dims,
    sophia_config,
);
defer opt.deinit();


Step:

zig
// activations: forward pass activations per layer (for K-FAC statistics)
try opt.update(&gradients, &params, &activations, learning_rate);


Update procedure (per call):
1. Clone current gradients into a working buffer
2. For each KFAC block: update A and G statistics from activations/gradients
3. Every update_freq steps: precondition gradients with G⁻¹/² · grad · A⁻¹/²
4. Every hessian_update_freq steps: reestimate Hessian diagonal via Hutchinson
5. Apply Sophia clipping: step = lr · g / max(|H_ii|, γ)
6. Call SFD update on the preconditioned gradient
7. Correct SFD eigenvalues via correctEigenvalues(lr)



 KFAC Blocks

Each linear layer gets a KFACBlock that maintains running estimates of:
 A = E[aₜ aₜᵀ] — covariance of layer inputs (activations)
 G = E[gₜ gₜᵀ] — covariance of layer output gradients

zig
var block = try KFACBlock.init(allocator, input_dim, output_dim, damping);
defer block.deinit();

// Update running statistics (EMA with α=0.95)
try block.updateStatistics(&activations, &gradients);

// Precondition a gradient: grad ← G⁻¹/² · grad · A⁻¹/²
try block.preconditionGradient(&grad);


The inverse matrix square root is computed via Schulz iteration (5 Newton steps):


Y_{k+1} = (1/2) · Y_k · (3I − M · Y_k²)


> ⚠️ Known bug: computeInverseSqrt contains a doublefree on the Y variable
> during the update loop. See .



 Gradient Flow Controller

Stabilizes gradient flow each step through three operations:

1. Spectral normalization of weights — scales each weight matrix so its largest
   singular value ≤ 1.0, enforcing a global Lipschitz constant
2. Gradient norm clipping — clips each gradient tensor's L2 norm to gradient_clip_norm
3. Gradient standardization — normalizes each gradient tensor to zero mean and unit
   variance (batch normalization for gradients)

zig
var controller = GradientFlowController.init();

// Pass gradient tensors and their corresponding weight matrices
try controller.stabilizeGradients(gradients_slice, weights_slice, allocator);


The SpectralNormalizer also exposes a Lipschitz regularization term:

zig
const regularized_loss = normalizer.lipschitzRegularization(
    loss,
    spectral_norms_slice,
    lambda,  // regularization strength
);




 MARS Variance Reducer

Implements SVRGstyle variance reduction: maintains a snapshot of reference
gradients and corrects stochastic minibatch gradients:


g_VR = g_current − g_ref_mini + g_ref_full
g_VR = momentum · g_VR + (1 − momentum) · g_current


zig
var reducer = try MARSVarianceReducer.init(allocator, param_shapes);
defer reducer.deinit();

// Update full-batch reference snapshot
try reducer.updateReferenceGradients(full_batch_grads);

// Get variance-reduced gradient for parameter i
const vr_grad = try reducer.varianceReducedGradient(
    &current_grad, &reference_grad, param_idx
);


Default: snapshot_freq = 100, momentum = 0.9.



 Reversible Optimizer State

Supports memoryefficient backpropagation through reversible architectures
(e.g., RevNet, iRevNet) using an adaptive cache/recompute policy.

zig
var rev_state = ReversibleOptimizerState.init(allocator);
defer rev_state.deinit();

// Decide whether to cache or recompute layer i
const recompute = rev_state.shouldRecompute(
    layer_idx,
    computation_cost,
    memory_cost,
    available_memory_bytes,
);

// Full reversible backward pass
const grad_inputs = try rev_state.backwardPassReversible(
    forward_outputs,
    &grad_output,
);


Cache Policies:

| Policy | Behavior |
|||
| .cache_all | Always store activations in memory |
| .recompute_all | Always recompute (minimal memory) |
| .adaptive | Recompute if compute_cost < memory_cost × threshold |



 Learning Rate Schedulers

zig
var scheduler = LRScheduler.init(
    allocator,
    .cosine_annealing,   // schedule type
    0.001,               // base LR
    warmup_steps,
    total_steps,
);

// Returns LR for the current step, then advances internal counter
const lr = try scheduler.getLearningRate(optional_hessian_tensor);


Available schedules:

| Type | Formula |
|||
| cosine_annealing | lr_min + (lr_base − lr_min) · ½(1 + cos(π · progress)) |
| cosine_annealing_with_warmup | Identical + warmup phase |
| polynomial_decay | lr_base · (1 − progress)² |
| exponential_decay | lr_base · 0.96^(step / 1000) |
| one_cycle | Linear ramp up to lr_max, cosine decay to lr_min |
| sophia_style | lr_base / √(median(H_diag) + ε) — Hessianadaptive |

For sophia_style, pass a Hessian diagonal tensor to getLearningRate. The schedule
sorts Hessian values and uses the median as a curvature proxy.



 Mixed Precision Trainer

Maintains FP32 master weights alongside lowerprecision working copies, with
gradient accumulation and dynamic loss scaling.

zig
const mp_config = MixedPrecisionConfig{
    .use_fp4                    = true,
    .use_fp8                    = true,
    .use_fp16                   = true,
    .master_weights_precision   = .fp32,
    .gradient_accumulation_steps = 4,
    .loss_scale                 = 1024.0,
    .dynamic_loss_scaling       = true,
};

var trainer = try MixedPrecisionTrainer.init(allocator, weight_shapes, mp_config);
defer trainer.deinit();

// After accumulating gradients for gradient_accumulation_steps mini-batches:
try trainer.updateWeights(learning_rate);


Precision selection priority: FP4 → FP8 → FP16 (for working weights).

Dynamic Loss Scaler (DynamicLossScaler):
 Detects NaN/Inf gradients (overflow)
 On overflow: scale ×= 0.5, reset counter
 After 2000 overflowfree steps: scale ×= 2.0
 Clamped to [1.0, 65536.0]



 B200 GPU Optimizations

Targets the NVIDIA Blackwell B200 architecture specifically.

Configuration:

zig
const b200_config = B200OptimizationConfig{
    .use_fp4_tensor_cores   = true,
    .use_tensor_memory      = true,
    .nvlink_bandwidth_tbps  = 1.8,   // NVLink 5.0 bandwidth (TB/s)
    .hbm_bandwidth_tbps     = 8.0,   // HBM3e bandwidth (TB/s)
    .decompression_engine   = true,
    .multi_instance_gpu     = false,
    .l2_cache_size_mb       = 50,
    .tmem_size_mb           = 32,    // On-chip Tensor Memory
};


Memory Manager — places hot tensors (access frequency > 10) into the simulated
32 MB TMEM pool, and automatically FP4compresses FP32 tensors when the decompression
engine is enabled:

zig
var mem_manager = try B200MemoryManager.init(allocator, b200_config);
defer mem_manager.deinit();

try mem_manager.optimizeMemoryAccess(tensor_ptrs, access_frequency_array);


Kernel Optimizer — fuses matmul → add → activation chains into a single
fused_gemm_bias_act operation, and selects optimal precision per operation:

zig
var kernel_opt = B200KernelOptimizer.init(b200_config);

// Fuse operation sequences
const fused = try kernel_opt.fuseOperations(operations_slice, allocator);
defer fused.deinit();

// Precision selection: FP4 for matmuls >1M elements, FP8 for >100K, else FP16
const precision = kernel_opt.selectOptimalPrecision(.matmul, tensor_element_count);




 Bayesian Hyperparameter Optimizer

Gaussian Processbased hyperparameter search using Expected Improvement as
the acquisition function.

zig
const search_space = HyperparameterSpace{
    .lr_min           = 1e-6,
    .lr_max           = 1e-2,
    .beta1_min        = 0.85,
    .beta1_max        = 0.95,
    .beta2_min        = 0.99,
    .beta2_max        = 0.9999,
    .weight_decay_min = 0.0,
    .weight_decay_max = 0.1,
};

var bayes_opt = try BayesianOptimizer.init(allocator, search_space);
defer bayes_opt.deinit();

// Exploration: first 5 calls return random configs
const suggested = try bayes_opt.suggestNext();

// After running a trial:
try bayes_opt.observe(suggested, validation_loss);

// Access the best configuration found so far:
const best = bayes_opt.best_params;


The GP uses an RBF (squared exponential) kernel:


k(x₁, x₂) = σ² · exp(−‖x₁ − x₂‖² / (2 · l²))


with default σ² = 1.0, l = 0.1, noise_variance = 0.01. Candidate selection
evaluates 100 random samples per call.



 Performance Monitor

Tracks perstep metrics including GPU telemetry (simulated via B200Profiler):

zig
var monitor = try PerformanceMonitor.init(allocator, true /* enable telemetry */);
defer monitor.deinit();

// Record a training step
try monitor.recordStep(
    loss,
    learning_rate,
    grad_norm,
    param_norm,
    step_time_ms,
);

// Generate a summary report
const report = try monitor.generateReport();
// report.average_loss, report.throughput_steps_per_sec,
// report.average_gpu_utilization, report.average_tensor_core_utilization, ...


Tracked metrics: training loss, validation loss, learning rate, gradient norm,
parameter norm, step time (ms), GPU utilization (%), memory usage (GB),
Tensor Core utilization (%), NVLink bandwidth utilization (%).



 Usage Examples

 Minimal SFD Training Loop

zig
const std = @import("std");
const optimizer_lib = @import("optimizer.zig");

pub fn main() !void {
    var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
    defer _ = gpa.deinit();
    const allocator = gpa.allocator();

    const param_size: usize = 1024;

    var params = try optimizer_lib.Tensor.zeros(allocator, &[_]usize{param_size});
    defer params.deinit();
    try params.fillRandomNormal(0.0, 0.02);

    var optimizer = try optimizer_lib.SFD.init(allocator, param_size);
    defer optimizer.deinit();

    var scheduler = optimizer_lib.LRScheduler.init(
        allocator, .cosine_annealing, 1e-3, 100, 10_000
    );

    var step: usize = 0;
    while (step < 10_000) : (step += 1) {
        // Compute gradients (user-provided)
        var grads = try computeGradients(allocator, &params);
        defer grads.deinit();

        const lr = try scheduler.getLearningRate(null);
        try optimizer.update(&grads, &params, lr);
    }

    try optimizer.saveState("model.bin");
}


 SophiaSOAP with Mixed Precision

zig
const layer_dims = [_]usize{
    [_]usize{ 768, 768 },
    [_]usize{ 768, 3072 },
    [_]usize{ 3072, 768 },
};

var sophia_opt = try SophiaSOAPOptimizer.init(
    allocator, total_params, &layer_dims, .{}
);
defer sophia_opt.deinit();

const mp_config = MixedPrecisionConfig{};
var mp_trainer = try MixedPrecisionTrainer.init(allocator, weight_shapes, mp_config);
defer mp_trainer.deinit();

var step: usize = 0;
while (step < total_steps) : (step += 1) {
    const lr = try scheduler.getLearningRate(null);

    try sophia_opt.update(&gradients, &params, &activations, lr);
    mp_trainer.loss_scaler.update(gradient_slice);

    if (step % mp_config.gradient_accumulation_steps == 0) {
        try mp_trainer.updateWeights(lr);
    }
}




 Known Issues & Limitations

> These are identified bugs in the current implementation that must be addressed
> before production use.

|  | Location | Description | Severity |
|||||
| 1 | KFACBlock.computeInverseSqrt | Doublefree: defer Y.deinit() fires after Y = Y_new reassignment | 🔴 Critical |
| 2 | SophiaSOAP.updateHessianDiagonal | EMA update h.* = α·h + (1−α)·h is a noop — Hessian never changes | 🔴 Critical |
| 3 | SophiaSOAP.updateHessianDiagonal | params_plus/params_minus are computed but never used in the finitedifference formula | 🟠 High |
| 4 | Tensor.copyFromWithCast | Does not perform any type casting; simply calls copyFrom | 🟡 Medium |
| 5 | fillRandomNormal / fillRademacher | PRNG seeded with std.time.timestamp() — same seed if called within the same second | 🟡 Medium |
| 6 | B200Profiler.captureGPUMetrics | Returns hardcoded values (85% GPU, 90% TC util) — no real GPU integration | 🔵 Info |
| 7 | convertToFP4 | Only sets a flag; performs no actual quantization | 🔵 Info |



 Memory Management

All types follow Zig's explicit allocator pattern. Every struct that allocates memory
exposes a matching deinit() method. Use errdefer to handle partial initialization
failures:

zig
var tensor = try Tensor.init(allocator, dims);
errdefer tensor.deinit();
// ... further initialization that might fail


Supported allocator backends:

| Allocator | Constructor |
|||
| std.mem.Allocator (any) | Tensor.init(allocator, dims) |
| core_memory.ArenaAllocator | Tensor.initWithArena(&arena, dims) |
| core_memory.PoolAllocator | Tensor.initWithPool(&pool, dims) |
| core_memory.SlabAllocator | Tensor.initWithSlab(&slab, dims) |
| core_memory.BuddyAllocator | Tensor.initWithBuddy(&buddy, dims) |

The same allocator variants are available for SFD (initWithArena, initWithPool,
initWithBuddy).



 Testing

The file includes a suite of builtin Zig unit tests:

sh
zig test optimizer.zig


Covered tests:

| Test | What it verifies |
|||
| SFD init and deinit | Correct initialization of internal buffers |
| SFD update | Parameter is modified after a gradient step |
| Tensor clone | Deep copy preserves values |
| KFACBlock init | Damping factor stored correctly |
| SpectralNormalizer | Power iteration count stored correctly |
| LRScheduler cosine annealing | LR is within [0, base_lr] range |
| BayesianOptimizer init | Suggested config is within search space bounds |
| SophiaSOAPOptimizer init | SFD suboptimizer initialized correctly |



